### How was the pre-Java 5 memory model broken?

The original JMM, defined in the Java Language Specification first edition, was underspecified in critical ways. The `volatile` keyword prevented the JVM from caching a variable's value (it forced reads from main memory), but it did not prevent instruction reordering around the volatile access. This made double-checked locking unreliable.

```java
// Pre-Java 5: BROKEN double-checked locking
public class BrokenSingleton {
    private static volatile BrokenSingleton instance;  // volatile in pre-5

    public static BrokenSingleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (BrokenSingleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new BrokenSingleton();
                    // Pre-Java 5: CPU could reorder to:
                    // 1. Allocate memory
                    // 2. Assign reference to instance (non-null now!)
                    // 3. Run constructor (fields still uninitialized!)
                    // Another thread sees non-null instance but null fields!
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Final fields also had no visibility guarantee. A `final String name = "Rajesh"` field could appear as null to another thread that obtained a reference to the object, because the JVM could reorder the publication of the reference ahead of the final field write.

### What did Java 5 (JSR-133) fix?

JSR-133 rewrote the memory model completely. The key fixes were:

- Volatile now provides a full happens-before edge. A volatile write inserts STORE-STORE and STORE-LOAD barriers that prevent any prior writes from being reordered past the volatile write. This made double-checked locking safe with volatile.
- Final fields are frozen at the end of the constructor. Any thread that sees a properly published reference to the object is guaranteed to see the fully initialized final fields.
- The happens-before framework was formally defined with all seven rules.
- `java.util.concurrent` was added: `Executors`, `ReentrantLock`, `AtomicInteger`, `ConcurrentHashMap`, `CountDownLatch`, `Semaphore`, and more.

```java
package com.bank.service;

// Java 5+: SAFE double-checked locking with volatile
public class ConnectionPoolSingleton {
    private static volatile ConnectionPoolSingleton instance;
    private final String poolName;
    private final int maxConnections;

    private ConnectionPoolSingleton() {
        this.poolName = "NBI-HikariPool";
        this.maxConnections = 20;
    }

    public static ConnectionPoolSingleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (ConnectionPoolSingleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new ConnectionPoolSingleton();
                    // Java 5+: volatile write PREVENTS reordering
                    // Constructor completes BEFORE reference is published
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;  // volatile read: guaranteed fully constructed
    }
}
```

### What did Java 9 add with VarHandle?

Java 9 introduced `java.lang.invoke.VarHandle` to provide fine-grained memory access modes, replacing the internal `sun.misc.Unsafe` class for low-level operations. VarHandle offers four access modes with increasing ordering strength.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles;
import java.lang.invoke.VarHandle;

public class VarHandleDemo {
    private int counter;

    private static final VarHandle COUNTER;
    static {
        try {
            COUNTER = MethodHandles.lookup()
                    .findVarHandle(VarHandleDemo.class, "counter", int.class);
        } catch (ReflectiveOperationException e) {
            throw new ExceptionInInitializerError(e);
        }
    }

    public void demonstrateModes() {
        // Plain: no ordering, no atomicity guarantee for long/double
        COUNTER.set(this, 1);
        int plain = (int) COUNTER.get(this);

        // Opaque: no tearing (atomic for long/double), but no ordering
        COUNTER.setOpaque(this, 2);
        int opaque = (int) COUNTER.getOpaque(this);

        // Acquire/Release: one-directional barrier (cheaper than volatile)
        COUNTER.setRelease(this, 3);    // all prior writes visible to acquire reader
        int acquired = (int) COUNTER.getAcquire(this);  // sees release writes

        // Volatile: full happens-before (strongest, most expensive)
        COUNTER.setVolatile(this, 4);
        int vol = (int) COUNTER.getVolatile(this);

        // CAS with volatile semantics
        boolean swapped = COUNTER.compareAndSet(this, 4, 5);
    }
}
```

| Access Mode | Atomicity | Ordering | Cost on x86 | Cost on ARM |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Plain | No (long/double may tear) | None | Free | Free |
| Opaque | Yes | None | Free | Free |
| Acquire/Release | Yes | One-directional | Free | Barrier |
| Volatile | Yes | Full bidirectional | MFENCE | Full barrier |

On x86 (Intel/AMD), acquire/release is free because x86 has a strong memory model. On ARM and POWER (used in some cloud servers), acquire/release saves the cost of a full barrier, which can be significant in tight loops.

### What changed in Java 21?

Java 21 introduced virtual threads and removed biased locking.

Virtual threads are lightweight threads managed by the JVM. A virtual thread uses only about 1 KB of stack (compared to 1 MB for a platform thread). When a virtual thread blocks on IO, the JVM unmounts it from its carrier (platform) thread, freeing the carrier for other virtual threads. This means blocking is cheap and you can create millions of virtual threads.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class VirtualThreadDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) {
                final int accountId = i;
                executor.submit(() -> {
                    // Each virtual thread blocks on DB call
                    // JVM unmounts it from carrier, carrier serves others
                    String balance = queryBalance("ACCT-" + accountId);
                    return balance;
                });
            }
        }  // waits for all tasks to complete
    }

    static String queryBalance(String accountId) {
        try { Thread.sleep(100); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }
        return "50000.00";
    }
}
```

Biased locking was removed because the optimization (skipping CAS for uncontended single-thread locks) had diminishing returns on modern hardware and added significant complexity to the JVM's lock implementation.

### What should a senior engineer know?

Application developers should use volatile and synchronized for almost all concurrency needs. VarHandle is intended for framework and library authors who need to squeeze out every nanosecond in lock-free algorithms. The acquire/release mode is the sweet spot for advanced lock-free code: it provides the ordering guarantees needed by most algorithms at lower cost than volatile on ARM architectures.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Is double-checked locking safe without volatile in Java 5+? No. The JSR-133 fix requires volatile on the instance field. Without it, the old reordering problem remains.
- Can VarHandle replace AtomicInteger? Technically yes, and in fact AtomicInteger uses VarHandle internally since Java 9. But AtomicInteger is a cleaner API for application code.
- Do virtual threads replace thread pools? For IO-bound work, yes. For CPU-bound work, you still want a bounded thread pool sized to the number of cores.
